ips (13137)]\\n\\nCystoscopic tools appear in the community as well: the rigid cystoscope is described as more invasive but capable of removing small tumors, connecting diagnostic visualization to interventional management in bladder cancer workups. [Data: Entities (5796); Relationships (13134)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Urine tumor-marker testing is framed as supportive rather than definitive, conceptually explained through tumor cell shedding into urine.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Urine tests for tumor markers are described as urine-based assays looking for tumor-related markers or cells shed into the urine, and are linked to bladder cancer evaluation as a way to detect cancer signals from tumors. [Data: Entities (5799); Relationships (13143)]\\n\\nThe dataset also includes an explanatory analogy (tumors shedding cells the way a dog sheds hair) to communicate the mechanism by which tumor-derived material can appear in urine and become detectable by marker tests. [Data: Entities (5785); Relationships (13094)]\\n\\nAt the same time, the broader bladder cancer description states that biomarker tests may provide clues but are not sufficiently reliable to establish diagnosis on their own, reinforcing that these tests complement rather than replace definitive diagnostic steps. [Data: Entities (2834, 5799)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Hematuria (blood in the urine) functions both as a clinical signal and as a recognized complication/side effect in bladder cancer-related procedures/therapies.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Blood in the urine is identified as a common presenting symptom in bladder cancer descriptions, connecting a patient-observable sign to the disease entity in this network. [Data: Entities (2834, 3311)]\\n\\nHematuria is also described as a potential complication after biopsy and a possible side effect of intravesical therapy, typically resolving within a few days when related to biopsy—linking symptom monitoring to post-procedural care considerations. [Data: Entities (3311)]\\n\\nThe relationship structure explicitly ties blood in the urine to urine as the manifestation site, supporting its role as a urine-detectable abnormality relevant to urinalysis and other urine-focused evaluation. [Data: Relationships (6530)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Advanced disease is defined by muscle invasion and metastatic spread to distant sites, shifting management toward systemic approaches.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Stage 4 metastatic bladder cancer is defined as cancer that has spread outside the bladder to distant sites in the body, capturing the network’s endpoint of dissemination beyond local bladder anatomy. [Data: Entities (5878, 5876); Relationships (13339)]\\n\\nThe dataset connects stage 4 disease to muscle-invasive bladder cancer, reflecting that metastatic disease is categorized within the muscle-invasive umbrella and signaling a generally more serious and complex clinical scenario than earlier stages. [Data: Relationships (13345)]\\n\\nThe general bladder cancer description notes that advanced/metastatic disease may involve systemic chemotherapy, immunotherapy (including checkpoint inhibitors), targeted therapy for eligible tumors, and clinical trials when appropriate—indicating a shift from local bladder-directed modalities to systemic treatment strategies. [Data: Entities (2834)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Genetic mutations and targeted therapy (FGFR3 → erdafitinib/Balversa) represent the precision-medicine branch of this community.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Genetic errors (mutations) are described as causing abnormal cells to generate many more cancer cells and avoid normal cell death, providing a mechanistic link between genetics and malignant growth within the community. [Data: Entities (5737); Relationships (12932)]\\n\\nA specific targeted-therapy pathway is captured: Balversa (erdafitinib) is used for bladder cancers driven by particular FGFR3 gene mutations